1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,840
Welcome to episode one of the System Design Workshop. By the end you'll be

2
00:00:04,840 --> 00:00:09,680
able to size a system with real numbers, explain latency, percentiles and the nines,

3
00:00:09,680 --> 00:00:13,829
scale a service out, and run a design interview step by step.

4
00:00:14,329 --> 00:00:19,122
We'll follow one running example most of the way: a photo-sharing app, like an

5
00:00:19,122 --> 00:00:23,916
early Instagram. Every time a slide lists steps or types, we'll apply each one

6
00:00:23,916 --> 00:00:25,627
to that app, with numbers.

7
00:00:26,127 --> 00:00:31,211
Keep the Part one notes open: each concept matches a section there, in order.

8
00:00:31,211 --> 00:00:34,117
Pause on any calculation and redo it yourself.

9
00:00:35,017 --> 00:00:40,281
System design is deciding which parts software needs, what each does, how they talk,

10
00:00:40,281 --> 00:00:45,545
and where the data lives, so the whole meets its goals for users, speed,

11
00:00:45,545 --> 00:00:46,673
uptime and cost.

12
00:00:47,173 --> 00:00:53,090
Coding asks how one function works. Design asks how twenty programs on two hundred

13
00:00:53,090 --> 00:00:59,007
machines cooperate without losing data. Like an architect: floors and fire exits, not bricks.

14
00:00:59,507 --> 00:01:04,670
A program that works for ten users can collapse at ten million because its

15
00:01:04,670 --> 00:01:09,832
shape is wrong. One database everyone queues for, one machine whose death kills the

16
00:01:09,832 --> 00:01:12,782
site. Better loops don't fix that. Design does.

17
00:01:13,282 --> 00:01:16,997
And two working designs can differ ten times over in their cloud bill.

18
00:01:17,897 --> 00:01:23,372
Design runs as a loop of five steps. Understand the goal. Estimate the load.

19
00:01:23,372 --> 00:01:28,847
Sketch the parts. Find the weak spots. Fix it and justify the fix. Then

20
00:01:28,847 --> 00:01:31,584
go round again as the system grows.

21
00:01:32,084 --> 00:01:36,202
That's easy to say and hard to picture, so let's run the loop once,

22
00:01:36,202 --> 00:01:38,261
step by step, on our photo app.

23
00:01:39,161 --> 00:01:43,782
Step one, understand the goal: what it must do, and how well. Our app

24
00:01:43,782 --> 00:01:48,404
lets people upload photos and scroll a feed. How well: the feed loads in

25
00:01:48,404 --> 00:01:53,026
under two hundred milliseconds for ninety-nine percent of requests, and the app is up

26
00:01:53,026 --> 00:01:55,337
ninety-nine point nine percent of the time.

27
00:01:55,837 --> 00:02:01,003
Step two, estimate the load. Ten million people a day each view fifty photos:

28
00:02:01,003 --> 00:02:06,170
about five thousand reads a second on average, fifteen thousand at the evening peak.

29
00:02:06,170 --> 00:02:09,122
We'll do this maths properly in concept nine.

30
00:02:09,622 --> 00:02:14,962
Step three, sketch the parts. Phones talk to a load balancer, which spreads requests

31
00:02:14,962 --> 00:02:20,303
over a few app servers. They store photo details in one database and the

32
00:02:20,303 --> 00:02:22,210
image files in object storage.

33
00:02:22,710 --> 00:02:28,280
Step four, find the weak spots. What runs out first? One database handles roughly

34
00:02:28,280 --> 00:02:33,850
one to ten thousand simple queries a second, so fifteen thousand reads at peak

35
00:02:33,850 --> 00:02:39,419
will swamp it. What takes everything down? That same database. If it dies, the

36
00:02:39,419 --> 00:02:40,215
app dies.

37
00:02:40,715 --> 00:02:45,610
Step five, fix and justify. Add a cache in front of the database, because

38
00:02:45,610 --> 00:02:50,505
there are two hundred and fifty reads for every write. Add a standby database

39
00:02:50,505 --> 00:02:55,400
in another building. Then say the cost: the cache can show slightly stale data,

40
00:02:55,400 --> 00:02:57,148
and the standby costs money.

41
00:02:57,648 --> 00:03:02,226
Then the loop restarts. At ten times the users, step four finds a new

42
00:03:02,226 --> 00:03:06,804
weak spot, maybe the cache or the network. A design is right for a

43
00:03:06,804 --> 00:03:08,112
given load, never finished.

44
00:03:09,012 --> 00:03:13,956
Your main tool is abstraction: a simple contract hiding the details. A remote call

45
00:03:13,956 --> 00:03:18,899
that looks like a local function. A database that looks like one table but

46
00:03:18,899 --> 00:03:20,312
is really many machines.

47
00:03:20,812 --> 00:03:26,590
Abstractions leak. Networks fail halfway, replicas lag, clocks drift. Our new cache is one:

48
00:03:26,590 --> 00:03:31,543
delete a photo, and the cache may show it for another minute.

49
00:03:32,043 --> 00:03:37,165
Every choice gives something up. More copies are safer but cost more and can

50
00:03:37,165 --> 00:03:39,361
disagree. Say the trade-off out loud.

51
00:03:39,861 --> 00:03:45,993
In the interview there's rarely one right answer. They grade the reasoning: questions, estimates,

52
00:03:45,993 --> 00:03:47,745
chosen parts, and trade-offs.

53
00:03:48,645 --> 00:03:54,060
A client is any program that asks: a browser, an app, or another server.

54
00:03:54,060 --> 00:03:59,475
A server waits for requests and answers them. On the web they speak H-T-T-P,

55
00:03:59,475 --> 00:04:04,889
a standard request and response format. Let's follow one request through the five numbered

56
00:04:04,889 --> 00:04:05,276
arrows.

57
00:04:05,776 --> 00:04:11,325
Arrow one. You open example dot com slash profile. The browser doesn't know where

58
00:04:11,325 --> 00:04:16,874
example dot com lives, so it asks D-N-S, the internet's phone book, and gets

59
00:04:16,874 --> 00:04:18,063
back an address.

60
00:04:18,563 --> 00:04:23,766
Arrow two. The browser connects to that address and sends a request: GET slash

61
00:04:23,766 --> 00:04:24,137
profile.

62
00:04:24,637 --> 00:04:29,706
Arrow three. The server reads it, sees you're user forty-two, and asks the database

63
00:04:29,706 --> 00:04:30,792
for your record.

64
00:04:31,292 --> 00:04:35,973
Arrow four. The database replies with your name, photo and bio.

65
00:04:36,473 --> 00:04:41,617
Arrow five. The server replies two hundred, O-K, with the page, and the browser

66
00:04:41,617 --> 00:04:46,762
draws it. Notice that in arrow three the server was itself a client. Inside

67
00:04:46,762 --> 00:04:50,804
big systems, servers are clients of other servers all the time.

68
00:04:51,704 --> 00:04:56,343
A thin client relies on the server: a basic web page that fetches everything.

69
00:04:56,343 --> 00:05:00,981
A thick client does a lot locally: a mobile game that only calls home

70
00:05:00,981 --> 00:05:02,306
to save your score.

71
00:05:02,806 --> 00:05:08,390
Normally the client pulls. A chat app needs the server to push a new

72
00:05:08,390 --> 00:05:13,177
message to you unasked, using WebSockets or server-sent events. That's episode two.

73
00:05:13,677 --> 00:05:18,717
A server is a role, not a box. One name, like api dot example

74
00:05:18,717 --> 00:05:22,676
dot com, is often hundreds of machines behind a load balancer.

75
00:05:23,176 --> 00:05:28,216
And the network between them is slow and unreliable compared with work inside one

76
00:05:28,216 --> 00:05:33,255
machine. Recap: almost everything on the web is a chain of request and response,

77
00:05:33,255 --> 00:05:34,695
often server to server.

78
00:05:35,595 --> 00:05:40,450
Nearly every web app uses the same eight parts. Here's each one's job in

79
00:05:40,450 --> 00:05:45,304
our photo app. The client renders the interface: the phone app showing your feed.

80
00:05:45,804 --> 00:05:51,148
The load balancer spreads requests over app servers and skips dead ones. Fifteen thousand

81
00:05:51,148 --> 00:05:54,583
feed requests a second get shared across the fleet.

82
00:05:55,083 --> 00:06:00,265
App servers apply business rules, like only followers may see a private photo. You

83
00:06:00,265 --> 00:06:03,967
run identical copies so one crash doesn't stop the site.

84
00:06:04,467 --> 00:06:10,541
The database stores data durably: who posted which photo, captions, likes. It's usually the

85
00:06:10,541 --> 00:06:14,012
hardest part to scale, because it holds state.

86
00:06:14,512 --> 00:06:19,707
A cache keeps hot data in RAM. Today's popular feed comes back in well

87
00:06:19,707 --> 00:06:23,790
under a millisecond, versus several milliseconds or more from the database.

88
00:06:24,290 --> 00:06:29,050
A queue plus workers runs slow jobs later. Making three thumbnail sizes of an

89
00:06:29,050 --> 00:06:32,790
upload goes on the queue, so the upload returns at once.

90
00:06:33,290 --> 00:06:39,408
Object storage holds big files by key, like photos slash forty-two slash beach. Databases

91
00:06:39,408 --> 00:06:41,593
are bad at large files.

92
00:06:42,093 --> 00:06:47,597
A C-D-N, content delivery network, keeps copies of the images near users, so someone

93
00:06:47,597 --> 00:06:50,742
in Sydney doesn't fetch every photo from Virginia.

94
00:06:51,642 --> 00:06:56,483
Now trace one upload. The phone sends the photo through the load balancer to

95
00:06:56,483 --> 00:07:01,324
an app server. The server saves the caption in the database, stores the file

96
00:07:01,324 --> 00:07:06,164
in object storage, and drops a make-thumbnails job on the queue. It replies at

97
00:07:06,164 --> 00:07:11,005
once. A worker picks up the job later and writes the thumbnails to object

98
00:07:11,005 --> 00:07:11,351
storage.

99
00:07:11,851 --> 00:07:17,197
Now trace one feed view. The app server asks the cache first. On a

100
00:07:17,197 --> 00:07:22,544
hit, done in under a millisecond. On a miss, it reads the database and

101
00:07:22,544 --> 00:07:27,508
fills the cache. The images themselves come from the C-D-N, not your servers.

102
00:07:28,008 --> 00:07:33,130
Instagram's early stack looked almost exactly like this. Add a box only when you

103
00:07:33,130 --> 00:07:38,251
can name its problem. A small internal tool may need just one server and

104
00:07:38,251 --> 00:07:38,983
one database.

105
00:07:39,883 --> 00:07:45,086
Don't store images in the database. Put files in object storage and keep only

106
00:07:45,086 --> 00:07:45,829
the key.

107
00:07:46,329 --> 00:07:51,520
Don't do slow work, like video encoding, inside the web request. Queue it.

108
00:07:52,020 --> 00:07:57,417
Interview question: why not one server? It's a single point of failure with a

109
00:07:57,417 --> 00:08:02,814
hard capacity limit. Stateless app servers behind a load balancer let you add machines

110
00:08:02,814 --> 00:08:03,970
and survive crashes.

111
00:08:04,470 --> 00:08:09,656
And add a queue when work is slow, can wait, or arrives in bursts.

112
00:08:09,656 --> 00:08:11,879
Recap: each box solves one problem.

113
00:08:12,779 --> 00:08:18,383
Three kinds of speed. Latency is how long one request takes, in milliseconds or

114
00:08:18,383 --> 00:08:18,783
microseconds.

115
00:08:19,283 --> 00:08:21,907
Throughput is how many requests finish per second.

116
00:08:22,407 --> 00:08:26,902
Bandwidth is the most a link can carry per second, in bits. The width

117
00:08:26,902 --> 00:08:27,865
of the pipe.

118
00:08:28,365 --> 00:08:33,425
On a motorway, latency is one car's journey time, throughput is cars actually arriving

119
00:08:33,425 --> 00:08:36,678
per hour, and bandwidth is the number of lanes.

120
00:08:37,178 --> 00:08:43,631
Strictly, response time is what the client feels: network both ways, plus queueing, plus

121
00:08:43,631 --> 00:08:47,780
service time. In interviews latency usually means response time.

122
00:08:48,280 --> 00:08:53,687
Latency and bandwidth are independent. A satellite link has huge bandwidth and six hundred

123
00:08:53,687 --> 00:08:59,095
millisecond round trips. More bandwidth won't speed up a small A-P-I call. And throughput

124
00:08:59,095 --> 00:09:04,503
sets fleet size: fifty thousand requests a second at a thousand per server needs

125
00:09:04,503 --> 00:09:06,048
at least fifty servers.

126
00:09:06,948 --> 00:09:12,853
Every request picks up four kinds of delay. Propagation: the time for the signal

127
00:09:12,853 --> 00:09:18,758
to travel. Transmission: the time to push the bytes onto the wire. Queueing: waiting

128
00:09:18,758 --> 00:09:21,711
behind other requests. Processing: the real work.

129
00:09:22,211 --> 00:09:27,550
Load links them. At low load latency is flat. Near full use, requests wait

130
00:09:27,550 --> 00:09:32,889
for each other and latency explodes while throughput levels off. That's why teams run

131
00:09:32,889 --> 00:09:38,228
at fifty to seventy percent at peak. Let's put real numbers on all four.

132
00:09:39,128 --> 00:09:43,646
Imagine a user in London opening a photo page served from New York. The

133
00:09:43,646 --> 00:09:48,164
page carries a one megabyte photo, and the user is on a hundred megabit

134
00:09:48,164 --> 00:09:48,487
connection.

135
00:09:48,987 --> 00:09:54,661
Propagation. Light in fibre covers about two hundred kilometres per millisecond. London to New

136
00:09:54,661 --> 00:10:00,336
York is fifty-six hundred kilometres, so at least twenty-eight milliseconds one way. Real cables

137
00:10:00,336 --> 00:10:05,200
wiggle and routers add delay, so a round trip is about seventy.

138
00:10:05,700 --> 00:10:11,566
Transmission. One megabyte is eight megabits. At a hundred megabits a second that takes

139
00:10:11,566 --> 00:10:17,432
eighty milliseconds. At one gigabit, just eight. This is the only term bandwidth helps.

140
00:10:17,932 --> 00:10:23,795
Queueing. Say the server is ninety percent busy at peak. Our request waits behind

141
00:10:23,795 --> 00:10:25,471
others, say thirty milliseconds.

142
00:10:25,971 --> 00:10:31,661
Processing. The database query and building the page, say fifteen milliseconds.

143
00:10:32,161 --> 00:10:37,857
Total: about a hundred and ninety-five milliseconds. Each delay has its own fix. A

144
00:10:37,857 --> 00:10:43,552
C-D-N copy in London cuts seventy to about ten. Compressing the photo to two

145
00:10:43,552 --> 00:10:49,247
hundred kilobytes cuts eighty to sixteen. Headroom shrinks the queue. An index speeds the

146
00:10:49,247 --> 00:10:49,654
query.

147
00:10:50,554 --> 00:10:55,497
Start with a latency budget. For a p ninety-nine target of two hundred milliseconds

148
00:10:55,497 --> 00:11:00,439
on the feed, give sixty to the network, twenty to the gateway, eighty to

149
00:11:00,439 --> 00:11:04,675
the service and database, forty as slack. Whoever overspends gets fixed first.

150
00:11:05,175 --> 00:11:10,454
Remove round trips. Say the feed screen makes five calls one after another from

151
00:11:10,454 --> 00:11:15,732
London to Virginia, at seventy milliseconds each: three hundred and fifty milliseconds. Batch them

152
00:11:15,732 --> 00:11:20,633
into one call and it's seventy. Reuse connections so you don't repeat handshakes.

153
00:11:21,133 --> 00:11:27,198
Move bytes closer with C-D-Ns. Serve hot reads from memory. Defer work like thumbnails

154
00:11:27,198 --> 00:11:28,497
onto the queue.

155
00:11:28,997 --> 00:11:35,184
Then shrink payloads, index queries, keep headroom, and cut the tail with deadlines and

156
00:11:35,184 --> 00:11:37,393
hedged requests. Measure every hop.

157
00:11:37,893 --> 00:11:43,853
Name each lever's price: caches go stale, replicas cost money, async work shows results

158
00:11:43,853 --> 00:11:44,278
later.

159
00:11:44,778 --> 00:11:50,278
Pause and think: a user in Australia is slow against servers in Virginia. Will

160
00:11:50,278 --> 00:11:51,456
more bandwidth help?

161
00:11:52,356 --> 00:11:59,144
Barely. The round trip is about two hundred milliseconds. Cut round trips: a nearby

162
00:11:59,144 --> 00:12:03,992
C-D-N, fewer sequential calls, reused connections, maybe a regional deployment.

163
00:12:04,492 --> 00:12:09,410
Don't mix bits and bytes. A hundred megabits per second moves at most twelve

164
00:12:09,410 --> 00:12:11,518
and a half megabytes per second.

165
00:12:12,018 --> 00:12:17,405
Batching raises throughput but adds latency: the first write waits for the other ninety-nine.

166
00:12:17,905 --> 00:12:23,656
Recap: a cargo ship has high throughput and high latency. A motorbike courier is

167
00:12:23,656 --> 00:12:24,478
the reverse.

168
00:12:25,378 --> 00:12:30,522
A percentile is the value below which that percentage of measurements fall. A p

169
00:12:30,522 --> 00:12:35,667
ninety-five of three hundred milliseconds means ninety-five percent finished within three hundred. p fifty

170
00:12:35,667 --> 00:12:36,769
is the median.

171
00:12:37,269 --> 00:12:39,789
p ninety: one request in ten is slower.

172
00:12:40,289 --> 00:12:42,587
p ninety-five: one in twenty.

173
00:12:43,087 --> 00:12:44,864
p ninety-nine: one in a hundred.

174
00:12:45,364 --> 00:12:49,907
p ninety-nine point nine: one in a thousand. This slow end of the graph

175
00:12:49,907 --> 00:12:51,205
is called tail latency.

176
00:12:52,105 --> 00:12:57,877
Here's a real-looking request log. Ten feed requests: nine take ten milliseconds, and one

177
00:12:57,877 --> 00:13:03,649
hits a garbage-collection pause and takes a thousand. To read percentiles, sort them and

178
00:13:03,649 --> 00:13:04,473
count positions.

179
00:13:04,973 --> 00:13:10,350
The mean adds them up, one thousand and ninety, and divides by ten: a

180
00:13:10,350 --> 00:13:15,726
hundred and nine milliseconds. No request took a hundred and nine. The average describes

181
00:13:15,726 --> 00:13:16,110
nobody.

182
00:13:16,610 --> 00:13:22,211
p fifty, the median, sits in the middle, the fifth and sixth values: ten

183
00:13:22,211 --> 00:13:24,611
milliseconds. The typical user is happy.

184
00:13:25,111 --> 00:13:29,152
p ninety is the ninth value out of ten: still ten milliseconds.

185
00:13:29,652 --> 00:13:34,714
p ninety-five lands past the ninth value, on the tenth: a thousand milliseconds.

186
00:13:35,214 --> 00:13:40,661
p ninety-nine is the tenth value too: a full second. So one user in

187
00:13:40,661 --> 00:13:46,108
ten waited a second, and only the high percentiles show it. Real latency is

188
00:13:46,108 --> 00:13:51,555
skewed like this, by pauses, cache misses and retries. Percentiles show the shape. Averages

189
00:13:51,555 --> 00:13:52,333
flatten it.

190
00:13:53,233 --> 00:13:57,683
The tail matters more than it looks. A heavy user who loads a hundred

191
00:13:57,683 --> 00:14:02,132
pages a day hits your p ninety-nine about once a day. And heavy users

192
00:14:02,132 --> 00:14:03,721
are often your best customers.

193
00:14:04,221 --> 00:14:09,205
Worse is fan-out. Imagine photo search: one user request asks a hundred search servers

194
00:14:09,205 --> 00:14:14,189
in parallel and waits for all of them. Each server is slow just one

195
00:14:14,189 --> 00:14:15,613
percent of the time.

196
00:14:16,113 --> 00:14:20,565
The chance all hundred are fast is point nine nine to the power of

197
00:14:20,565 --> 00:14:25,017
a hundred, about thirty-seven percent. So about sixty-three percent of searches hit a slow

198
00:14:25,017 --> 00:14:29,469
server. One in a hundred per server becomes two in three for the user.

199
00:14:29,469 --> 00:14:32,012
That's from Google's paper, The Tail at Scale.

200
00:14:32,912 --> 00:14:39,786
Monitoring uses histograms, counts per latency bucket, to estimate percentiles without storing every value.

201
00:14:40,286 --> 00:14:45,361
Never average percentiles across servers. Merge the histograms instead.

202
00:14:45,861 --> 00:14:51,614
Cut the tail with hedged requests. Say the search server's p ninety-five is fifty

203
00:14:51,614 --> 00:14:57,367
milliseconds. If no reply after fifty, ask a second replica and take whichever answers

204
00:14:57,367 --> 00:15:03,120
first. That costs only about five percent extra requests, and rescues the slow ones.

205
00:15:03,120 --> 00:15:05,175
Add timeouts and limit fan-out.

206
00:15:05,675 --> 00:15:11,071
Beware coordinated omission: a load tester that waits on each slow response sends less

207
00:15:11,071 --> 00:15:13,384
traffic exactly then, hiding the tail.

208
00:15:13,884 --> 00:15:19,858
Interview question: your service's p ninety-nine is fine but users still complain. Why? Each

209
00:15:19,858 --> 00:15:25,831
page calls many services, so the chance of at least one slow call per

210
00:15:25,831 --> 00:15:31,805
page is far above one percent. Look at end-to-end latency. Recap: percentiles, not averages.

211
00:15:32,705 --> 00:15:38,687
Little's law holds for any stable system, where items arrive, spend some time, and

212
00:15:38,687 --> 00:15:41,251
leave. L equals lambda times W.

213
00:15:41,751 --> 00:15:46,720
L is the number of items inside at once, like requests in flight. Lambda

214
00:15:46,720 --> 00:15:51,689
is the arrival rate, which in a stable system equals throughput. W is the

215
00:15:51,689 --> 00:15:56,657
time each item spends inside: the latency. Know any two and you get the

216
00:15:56,657 --> 00:15:57,012
third.

217
00:15:57,912 --> 00:16:01,768
Picture a coffee shop. Let's find each of the three letters.

218
00:16:02,268 --> 00:16:05,890
Lambda, the arrival rate: two customers walk in every minute.

219
00:16:06,390 --> 00:16:11,429
W, the time inside: each customer queues, gets a drink and sits for ten

220
00:16:11,429 --> 00:16:11,789
minutes.

221
00:16:12,289 --> 00:16:18,261
So L, the number inside on average, is two times ten: twenty people. That's

222
00:16:18,261 --> 00:16:21,673
true whatever the barista does. It's just counting.

223
00:16:22,173 --> 00:16:27,473
Now the barista slows down and each visit stretches to fifteen minutes. Same arrivals,

224
00:16:27,473 --> 00:16:32,773
so thirty people inside. With twenty seats, ten are standing. Slower service means more

225
00:16:32,773 --> 00:16:34,288
people in the building.

226
00:16:34,788 --> 00:16:39,712
Run it backwards. The shop has twenty seats and visits last ten minutes, so

227
00:16:39,712 --> 00:16:44,636
it can serve at most twenty divided by ten: two customers a minute. That's

228
00:16:44,636 --> 00:16:47,098
the same sum we'll do for servers.

229
00:16:47,998 --> 00:16:53,204
Now swap customers for requests. Our feed service gets a thousand requests a second,

230
00:16:53,204 --> 00:16:58,410
and each takes point two seconds. A thousand times point two is two hundred

231
00:16:58,410 --> 00:17:03,617
in flight. So you need at least two hundred threads or connections, plus headroom,

232
00:17:03,617 --> 00:17:04,733
or requests queue.

233
00:17:05,233 --> 00:17:10,756
Run it backwards for a database pool. Twenty connections, each query takes five milliseconds,

234
00:17:10,756 --> 00:17:16,279
which is point zero zero five seconds. Twenty divided by point zero zero five:

235
00:17:16,279 --> 00:17:19,040
at most four thousand queries a second.

236
00:17:19,540 --> 00:17:25,139
And here's why slow dependencies hurt. Keep the twenty connections, but the database slows

237
00:17:25,139 --> 00:17:30,738
to ten milliseconds a query. Max throughput halves, to two thousand. Same hardware, half

238
00:17:30,738 --> 00:17:31,538
the capacity.

239
00:17:32,038 --> 00:17:38,292
Two rules: the system must be stable, and convert milliseconds to seconds first. Pause:

240
00:17:38,292 --> 00:17:43,652
a hundred threads, each held two hundred and fifty milliseconds. Max throughput?

241
00:17:44,152 --> 00:17:49,731
A hundred divided by point two five: four hundred requests a second. Beyond that,

242
00:17:49,731 --> 00:17:51,723
requests queue and latency climbs.

243
00:17:52,623 --> 00:17:58,779
From Jeff Dean and Peter Norvig. Learn orders of magnitude, not digits. An L

244
00:17:58,779 --> 00:18:02,297
one cache read, inside the processor: one nanosecond.

245
00:18:02,797 --> 00:18:05,514
A RAM read: a hundred nanoseconds.

246
00:18:06,014 --> 00:18:11,746
A random four kilobyte read from a fast N-V-M-e S-S-D: twenty to a hundred

247
00:18:11,746 --> 00:18:12,156
microseconds.

248
00:18:12,656 --> 00:18:16,185
A round trip inside one data centre: half a millisecond.

249
00:18:16,685 --> 00:18:21,225
One megabyte read sequentially from S-S-D: point two to one millisecond.

250
00:18:21,725 --> 00:18:25,974
A hard disk seek: ten milliseconds, because an arm physically moves.

251
00:18:26,474 --> 00:18:29,853
London to New York round trip: seventy milliseconds.

252
00:18:30,353 --> 00:18:32,988
California to Europe: a hundred and fifty.

253
00:18:33,888 --> 00:18:38,269
Scale it up to human time. If an L one read took a second,

254
00:18:38,269 --> 00:18:42,650
a RAM read would take about two minutes, a data-centre round trip about six

255
00:18:42,650 --> 00:18:46,719
days, a disk seek four months, and London to New York two years.

256
00:18:47,619 --> 00:18:51,552
Let's use the table to price parts of one feed request in our photo

257
00:18:51,552 --> 00:18:51,833
app.

258
00:18:52,333 --> 00:18:57,695
The feed needs twenty pieces of data from Redis. Fetched one after another, each

259
00:18:57,695 --> 00:19:03,056
costs a data-centre round trip of half a millisecond: ten milliseconds. The RAM read

260
00:19:03,056 --> 00:19:07,269
inside Redis is nanoseconds. The trip is what you pay for.

261
00:19:07,769 --> 00:19:12,791
Batch them into one multi-get call and it's one round trip: half a millisecond.

262
00:19:12,791 --> 00:19:14,585
Twenty times faster, same data.

263
00:19:15,085 --> 00:19:20,380
Now a cache miss sends ten random reads to a database. On S-S-D, at

264
00:19:20,380 --> 00:19:23,783
about a hundred microseconds each, that's about one millisecond.

265
00:19:24,283 --> 00:19:29,686
If that database sat on spinning disks, ten seeks at ten milliseconds is a

266
00:19:29,686 --> 00:19:35,090
hundred milliseconds. Half our whole budget gone on disk arms. That's why databases moved

267
00:19:35,090 --> 00:19:35,861
to S-S-Ds.

268
00:19:36,361 --> 00:19:42,009
And a call to another continent adds seventy to a hundred and fifty milliseconds.

269
00:19:42,009 --> 00:19:47,657
Only moving data closer fixes that. Interview version: fifty sequential calls of a millisecond

270
00:19:47,657 --> 00:19:51,692
is fifty milliseconds before any real work. Batch or parallelise.

271
00:19:52,592 --> 00:19:57,297
RAM is a hundred to a thousand times faster than S-S-D for random reads,

272
00:19:57,297 --> 00:19:58,977
so caches live in RAM.

273
00:19:59,477 --> 00:20:04,292
A data-centre round trip costs as much as thousands of memory reads. A thousand

274
00:20:04,292 --> 00:20:08,418
sequential database calls cost at least half a second in trips alone.

275
00:20:08,918 --> 00:20:14,152
Ocean crossings cost seventy to a hundred and fifty milliseconds, and light isn't getting

276
00:20:14,152 --> 00:20:14,526
faster.

277
00:20:15,026 --> 00:20:23,043
Sequential beats random, so storage engines write append-only logs. Recap: memory is nanoseconds, S-S-D

278
00:20:23,043 --> 00:20:25,906
microseconds, network and disk milliseconds.

279
00:20:26,806 --> 00:20:31,461
A byte is eight bits, enough for one letter. Each unit is about a

280
00:20:31,461 --> 00:20:36,115
thousand times the last. Two to the ten is ten twenty-four, but estimates round

281
00:20:36,115 --> 00:20:40,105
it to a thousand. A kilobyte: a short post with its metadata.

282
00:20:40,605 --> 00:20:44,436
A megabyte: a high-quality photo, or a minute of M-P-three.

283
00:20:44,936 --> 00:20:49,127
A gigabyte: an hour of streamed H-D video is a few of these.

284
00:20:49,627 --> 00:20:55,025
A terabyte: a laptop drive. Our photo app takes in one of these every

285
00:20:55,025 --> 00:20:55,411
day.

286
00:20:55,911 --> 00:21:01,266
A petabyte: a large company's data warehouse. Our app reaches nearly two of these

287
00:21:01,266 --> 00:21:02,414
in five years.

288
00:21:03,314 --> 00:21:08,173
Two to the thirty-two is about four point three billion. That's every I-P version

289
00:21:08,173 --> 00:21:13,032
four address, and where a thirty-two bit I-D column runs out. A signed one

290
00:21:13,032 --> 00:21:15,461
runs out at about two billion rows.

291
00:21:15,961 --> 00:21:21,712
A timestamp is eight bytes, a U-U-I-D sixteen. A compressed photo is two hundred

292
00:21:21,712 --> 00:21:27,052
kilobytes to two megabytes. A minute of H-D video, twenty-five to sixty megabytes.

293
00:21:27,552 --> 00:21:31,942
A day is eighty-six thousand four hundred seconds. Round it to ten to the

294
00:21:31,942 --> 00:21:33,196
five, a hundred thousand.

295
00:21:33,696 --> 00:21:38,242
So one million per day is about twelve per second. For example, a shop

296
00:21:38,242 --> 00:21:42,464
taking three million orders a day handles about thirty-six a second on average.

297
00:21:42,964 --> 00:21:47,129
And a one gigabit network card moves a hundred and twenty-five megabytes per second

298
00:21:47,129 --> 00:21:50,104
at best, because there are eight bits in a byte.

299
00:21:51,004 --> 00:21:56,985
A back-of-the-envelope estimate finds the order of magnitude, and that decides the architecture. A

300
00:21:56,985 --> 00:22:02,965
hundred requests a second fits one server. A hundred thousand needs caching and sharding.

301
00:22:03,465 --> 00:22:09,699
Start from assumptions. D-A-U means daily active users. Multiply by actions per user to

302
00:22:09,699 --> 00:22:11,480
get requests per day.

303
00:22:11,980 --> 00:22:16,693
Divide by ten to the five to get average Q-P-S, queries per second.

304
00:22:17,193 --> 00:22:22,336
Multiply by two or three for the peak, because traffic bunches up in the

305
00:22:22,336 --> 00:22:25,275
evening. More for spiky products like ticket sales.

306
00:22:25,775 --> 00:22:31,521
Storage per day is new items times their size. Total storage is that times

307
00:22:31,521 --> 00:22:37,266
days kept, times replicas. And bandwidth is Q-P-S times response size. Say every assumption

308
00:22:37,266 --> 00:22:41,781
out loud. Now the photo app, one line at a time.

309
00:22:42,681 --> 00:22:48,761
Assumptions, said out loud: ten million daily users, each viewing fifty photos and uploading

310
00:22:48,761 --> 00:22:54,842
point two a day. Photos average five hundred kilobytes, metadata one kilobyte, and we

311
00:22:54,842 --> 00:22:56,580
keep everything five years.

312
00:22:57,080 --> 00:23:02,272
Reads. Ten million times fifty is five hundred million views a day. Divide by

313
00:23:02,272 --> 00:23:07,464
ten to the five: about five thousand Q-P-S average, fifteen thousand at a three

314
00:23:07,464 --> 00:23:08,205
times peak.

315
00:23:08,705 --> 00:23:13,560
Writes. Ten million times point two is two million uploads a day: about twenty

316
00:23:13,560 --> 00:23:18,414
per second, sixty at peak. That's two hundred and fifty reads per write, so

317
00:23:18,414 --> 00:23:20,841
caching and a C-D-N matter a lot.

318
00:23:21,341 --> 00:23:26,605
Storage per day. Two million times five hundred kilobytes is one terabyte of photos

319
00:23:26,605 --> 00:23:29,238
a day, plus two gigabytes of metadata.

320
00:23:29,738 --> 00:23:35,969
Five years. One terabyte times three sixty-five times five is about one point eight

321
00:23:35,969 --> 00:23:42,200
petabytes, times three with replicas. Photos go in object storage. Metadata, about three point

322
00:23:42,200 --> 00:23:44,870
six terabytes, fits a sharded database.

323
00:23:45,370 --> 00:23:50,880
Bandwidth. Five thousand views a second times five hundred kilobytes is two and a

324
00:23:50,880 --> 00:23:55,997
half gigabytes per second, about twenty gigabits. A strong case for a C-D-N.

325
00:23:56,897 --> 00:24:02,670
Now server count, the step interviewers love. Assume thirty thousand requests a second at

326
00:24:02,670 --> 00:24:08,444
peak. Each uses four milliseconds of C-P-U and takes fifty milliseconds end to end.

327
00:24:08,444 --> 00:24:14,217
Sixteen-core machines, three availability zones, and we must survive losing one. Step one: one

328
00:24:14,217 --> 00:24:19,578
core does a thousand divided by four, two hundred and fifty per second.

329
00:24:20,078 --> 00:24:24,501
Step two: times sixteen cores, four thousand per server at full load.

330
00:24:25,001 --> 00:24:30,811
Step three: keep C-P-U at sixty percent so queues stay short. Twenty-four hundred per

331
00:24:30,811 --> 00:24:31,226
server.

332
00:24:31,726 --> 00:24:37,349
Step four: thirty thousand divided by twenty-four hundred is twelve and a half. Round

333
00:24:37,349 --> 00:24:38,554
up to thirteen.

334
00:24:39,054 --> 00:24:44,419
Step five, a Little's law check: thirty thousand times point zero five is fifteen

335
00:24:44,419 --> 00:24:48,634
hundred in flight, about a hundred and fifteen per server. Fine.

336
00:24:49,134 --> 00:24:55,303
Step six, zone loss: thirteen must survive with two zones left, so seven per

337
00:24:55,303 --> 00:24:57,507
zone. Times three zones: twenty-one.

338
00:24:58,007 --> 00:25:03,665
Step seven: rolling deploys take about ten percent out at a time, so roughly

339
00:25:03,665 --> 00:25:04,473
twenty-three servers.

340
00:25:04,973 --> 00:25:10,501
Step eight, network: twenty kilobyte responses give about four point eight gigabits total, about

341
00:25:10,501 --> 00:25:15,238
a quarter gigabit per server. C-P-U is the limit, not the network.

342
00:25:16,138 --> 00:25:20,934
The naive answer, thirty thousand over four thousand, was eight. The ceiling and zone-loss

343
00:25:20,934 --> 00:25:23,675
rule explain the gap of about three times.

344
00:25:24,175 --> 00:25:28,646
C-P-U per request set the number. Halve it and you halve the fleet.

345
00:25:29,146 --> 00:25:34,301
Repeat per tier: caches are limited by memory, databases by disk and writes.

346
00:25:34,801 --> 00:25:41,196
Common mistakes: forgetting peak, replicas and index overhead, and false precision. Spend three to

347
00:25:41,196 --> 00:25:43,023
five minutes, out loud.

348
00:25:43,523 --> 00:25:49,654
Pause: a hundred million daily users, twenty requests each. Plan for what Q-P-S?

349
00:25:50,154 --> 00:25:54,488
Two billion a day over ten to the five is twenty thousand average, about

350
00:25:54,488 --> 00:25:58,821
sixty thousand at peak. For scale, WhatsApp's hundred billion messages a day is about

351
00:25:58,821 --> 00:26:00,059
a million per second.

352
00:26:00,959 --> 00:26:06,616
Availability is the share of time a system works correctly: uptime over total time,

353
00:26:06,616 --> 00:26:12,272
or successful requests over valid ones. Two nines, ninety-nine percent, allows three point six

354
00:26:12,272 --> 00:26:16,716
five days down a year, seven point two hours a month.

355
00:26:17,216 --> 00:26:21,841
Three nines, ninety-nine point nine: eight point seven six hours a year, forty-three minutes

356
00:26:21,841 --> 00:26:25,145
a month, about a minute and a half a day.

357
00:26:25,645 --> 00:26:30,430
Ninety-nine point nine five: four point three eight hours a year, about twenty-two minutes

358
00:26:30,430 --> 00:26:31,114
a month.

359
00:26:31,614 --> 00:26:36,340
Four nines: fifty-three minutes a year, four point three minutes a month. Less than

360
00:26:36,340 --> 00:26:41,066
a human needs to wake up and log in, so recovery must be automatic.

361
00:26:41,566 --> 00:26:47,044
Five nines: about five minutes a year. Each nine cuts downtime ten times, and

362
00:26:47,044 --> 00:26:50,565
usually costs far more than ten times the effort.

363
00:26:51,465 --> 00:26:56,557
Let's measure a real outage with this table. In February twenty seventeen, Amazon S-three

364
00:26:56,557 --> 00:27:00,558
in its biggest U-S region went down for about four hours.

365
00:27:01,058 --> 00:27:05,930
The cause was human. An engineer ran a routine command to remove a few

366
00:27:05,930 --> 00:27:10,803
servers, mistyped one input, and removed far more than intended. Thousands of websites that

367
00:27:10,803 --> 00:27:12,892
stored files there broke with it.

368
00:27:13,392 --> 00:27:17,642
Against a three nines yearly budget of eight point seven six hours, that one

369
00:27:17,642 --> 00:27:19,464
afternoon used about half the year.

370
00:27:19,964 --> 00:27:25,207
Against a monthly target of ninety-nine point nine, which allows forty-three minutes, four hours

371
00:27:25,207 --> 00:27:28,578
drops that month to about ninety-nine point four percent.

372
00:27:29,078 --> 00:27:34,340
Against four nines, fifty-three minutes a year, the budget was blown more than four

373
00:27:34,340 --> 00:27:37,346
times over. One bad afternoon decides your nines.

374
00:27:38,246 --> 00:27:43,381
Now, how parts combine. Parts in series must all work, so you multiply. A

375
00:27:43,381 --> 00:27:48,516
feed request goes through a load balancer at four nines, an app tier at

376
00:27:48,516 --> 00:27:53,652
three, and a database at three. Point nine nine nine nine times point nine

377
00:27:53,652 --> 00:27:58,787
nine nine times point nine nine nine: about ninety-nine point seven nine percent. Lower

378
00:27:58,787 --> 00:28:00,254
than the weakest link.

379
00:28:00,754 --> 00:28:05,610
Parts in parallel need only one to work. Two independent servers at ninety-nine percent

380
00:28:05,610 --> 00:28:10,466
are both down only one percent of one percent of the time: ninety-nine point

381
00:28:10,466 --> 00:28:13,934
nine nine. Two cheap two-nines machines make a four-nines pair.

382
00:28:14,434 --> 00:28:19,169
But only if failures are independent. Put both in one rack, and one power

383
00:28:19,169 --> 00:28:20,183
cut takes both.

384
00:28:21,083 --> 00:28:25,994
Going from three nines to four can mean several data centres and double the

385
00:28:25,994 --> 00:28:29,502
infrastructure. Many internal tools are fine at ninety-nine point five.

386
00:28:30,002 --> 00:28:35,249
Cloud S-L-As for single services are usually three or four nines. S-three is designed

387
00:28:35,249 --> 00:28:38,247
for four nines but its contract promises three.

388
00:28:38,747 --> 00:28:43,590
Planned maintenance still counts as downtime. Users don't care why it's down.

389
00:28:44,090 --> 00:28:49,938
Interview question: how do you beat any single machine's availability? Redundant copies in parallel

390
00:28:49,938 --> 00:28:55,787
across racks, zones or regions, with automatic failover and no single points of failure.

391
00:28:55,787 --> 00:29:00,800
Recap: three nines is forty-three minutes a month, four nines about four.

392
00:29:01,700 --> 00:29:07,116
Three terms, from most technical to most legal. An S-L-I, service level indicator, is

393
00:29:07,116 --> 00:29:07,890
a measurement.

394
00:29:08,390 --> 00:29:13,301
An S-L-O, service level objective, is an internal target for that measurement.

395
00:29:13,801 --> 00:29:20,237
An S-L-A, service level agreement, is a contract with customers, with penalties, usually service

396
00:29:20,237 --> 00:29:20,697
credits.

397
00:29:21,197 --> 00:29:26,303
Think of a pizza shop. The S-L-I is how long deliveries actually take. The

398
00:29:26,303 --> 00:29:31,408
S-L-O is the manager's ninety-five percent within thirty minutes. The S-L-A is the printed

399
00:29:31,408 --> 00:29:36,514
promise: over forty-five minutes and it's free. The S-L-A is looser, so missing the

400
00:29:36,514 --> 00:29:39,431
S-L-O warns you before you have to pay.

401
00:29:40,331 --> 00:29:45,726
Now one real service: our photo app's feed A-P-I, serving a hundred million requests

402
00:29:45,726 --> 00:29:46,496
a month.

403
00:29:46,996 --> 00:29:52,712
The S-L-I: the percentage of feed requests that succeed in under three hundred milliseconds,

404
00:29:52,712 --> 00:29:54,345
measured over thirty days.

405
00:29:54,845 --> 00:29:58,201
The S-L-O: ninety-nine point nine percent of them.

406
00:29:58,701 --> 00:30:04,151
The S-L-A: ninety-nine point five percent, or business customers get ten percent of their

407
00:30:04,151 --> 00:30:06,098
bill back. Looser on purpose.

408
00:30:06,598 --> 00:30:11,631
The error budget is one minus the S-L-O: point one percent. Of a hundred

409
00:30:11,631 --> 00:30:14,866
million requests, a hundred thousand may fail this month.

410
00:30:15,366 --> 00:30:20,691
Incident one. A bad deploy fails two percent of requests for an hour. An

411
00:30:20,691 --> 00:30:26,017
hour is about a hundred and thirty-nine thousand requests, so about twenty-eight hundred failed.

412
00:30:26,017 --> 00:30:30,582
That's under three percent of the budget. Roll back and keep shipping.

413
00:30:31,082 --> 00:30:36,885
Incident two. The database dies and the feed is down for thirty minutes: about

414
00:30:36,885 --> 00:30:42,688
seventy thousand failures, seventy percent of the budget. The team freezes risky launches and

415
00:30:42,688 --> 00:30:48,492
works on failover. Still inside the S-L-A, so no refunds. The warning came first.

416
00:30:49,392 --> 00:30:55,990
So the budget turns reliability into a shared decision. While budget remains, ship fast.

417
00:30:55,990 --> 00:31:02,588
When it's spent, freeze risky changes and fix reliability. The idea comes from Google's

418
00:31:02,588 --> 00:31:04,002
site reliability engineering.

419
00:31:04,502 --> 00:31:10,034
Rules of thumb: pick indicators users feel, like failed checkouts, not C-P-U. Never set

420
00:31:10,034 --> 00:31:15,565
an S-L-O of a hundred percent, because any change would violate it. Measure over

421
00:31:15,565 --> 00:31:20,307
a rolling thirty days, and alert when the budget burns too fast.

422
00:31:21,207 --> 00:31:27,302
Two measures of failure. M-T-B-F, mean time between failures, is how long a system

423
00:31:27,302 --> 00:31:33,398
runs before it breaks. Higher is better. M-T-T-R, mean time to repair or recover,

424
00:31:33,398 --> 00:31:37,316
is how long it stays broken. Lower is better.

425
00:31:37,816 --> 00:31:45,029
Availability is M-T-B-F divided by M-T-B-F plus M-T-T-R. There's also M-T-T-D, mean time to

426
00:31:45,029 --> 00:31:50,695
detect: how long before anyone notices. Detection is part of repair.

427
00:31:51,595 --> 00:31:56,960
Take our photo app's database server. On average it fails once every thousand hours,

428
00:31:56,960 --> 00:31:58,493
about every six weeks.

429
00:31:58,993 --> 00:32:06,148
Version one, manual recovery. Nobody notices for thirty minutes, until users complain. That's the

430
00:32:06,148 --> 00:32:07,170
detection time.

431
00:32:07,670 --> 00:32:13,548
Then an engineer logs in, diagnoses, and promotes the replica: another thirty minutes. So

432
00:32:13,548 --> 00:32:15,648
M-T-T-R is about an hour.

433
00:32:16,148 --> 00:32:21,686
Availability: a thousand over a thousand and one, about ninety-nine point nine percent.

434
00:32:22,186 --> 00:32:27,886
Version two, same failure rate. A health check spots it and automatic failover finishes

435
00:32:27,886 --> 00:32:29,107
in ten seconds.

436
00:32:29,607 --> 00:32:35,529
Availability: about ninety-nine point nine nine nine seven percent. Three nines to five, without

437
00:32:35,529 --> 00:32:41,451
the machine failing any less. At scale something is always failing, so cutting M-T-T-R

438
00:32:41,451 --> 00:32:43,566
is usually the cheaper lever.

439
00:32:44,466 --> 00:32:49,216
Three words people mix up. Availability: can I use it right now?

440
00:32:49,716 --> 00:32:55,382
Reliability: does it do the right thing over time? A system can be available

441
00:32:55,382 --> 00:32:58,216
but unreliable, answering fast with wrong data.

442
00:32:58,716 --> 00:33:04,354
Durability: will saved data survive crashes and dead disks? A system can be down

443
00:33:04,354 --> 00:33:07,576
but durable: offline for an hour, nothing lost.

444
00:33:08,476 --> 00:33:13,389
Think of a bank. The cash machine works when you walk up: availability.

445
00:33:13,889 --> 00:33:17,476
It gives the right amount and debits the right account: reliability.

446
00:33:17,976 --> 00:33:21,529
Your balance survives a flood at the branch: durability.

447
00:33:22,029 --> 00:33:28,326
A real number: S-three is designed for eleven nines of durability. Store ten million

448
00:33:28,326 --> 00:33:34,624
objects and you'd lose one about once in ten thousand years. But availability is

449
00:33:34,624 --> 00:33:40,921
only four nines. Separate promises, separate techniques: availability from redundancy and failover, durability from

450
00:33:40,921 --> 00:33:45,869
copies in separate places, writing to disk before confirming, and backups.

451
00:33:46,369 --> 00:33:51,979
And replication isn't backup. A bug that deletes your photos is faithfully copied to

452
00:33:51,979 --> 00:33:55,184
every replica. Only a backup brings them back.

453
00:33:55,684 --> 00:34:01,499
Pause: can a system be durable but not available? Yes. A database down for

454
00:34:01,499 --> 00:34:07,313
maintenance can't be read, but its data is safe on disk. Recap: usable now,

455
00:34:07,313 --> 00:34:09,389
correct over time, data survives.

456
00:34:10,289 --> 00:34:15,478
A fault is one part going wrong. A failure is the whole system not

457
00:34:15,478 --> 00:34:20,667
serving users. Fault tolerance stops faults becoming failures, like a twin-engine plane landing on

458
00:34:20,667 --> 00:34:25,855
one engine. Here are five kinds of fault, each in our photo app. Hardware:

459
00:34:25,855 --> 00:34:30,673
a disk in the database server dies. Defence: a replica on another machine.

460
00:34:31,173 --> 00:34:36,732
Software: a release leaks memory and app servers crash every few hours. Defence: tests,

461
00:34:36,732 --> 00:34:39,511
and rolling out to five percent first.

462
00:34:40,011 --> 00:34:46,697
Network: a link between two zones drops packets. Defence: timeouts, retries, and replicas in

463
00:34:46,697 --> 00:34:47,652
other zones.

464
00:34:48,152 --> 00:34:54,519
Human: someone pushes a config pointing every server at the wrong database. Defence: reviews,

465
00:34:54,519 --> 00:34:59,521
backups, and fast rollback. Big cloud outages are often this kind.

466
00:35:00,021 --> 00:35:06,173
Dependency: the third-party face-tagging A-P-I is slow. Defence: timeouts, a fallback that skips tagging,

467
00:35:06,173 --> 00:35:11,006
and a circuit breaker that stops calling it for a while.

468
00:35:11,906 --> 00:35:16,967
Faults also differ by how the broken part behaves. These are failure models. Each

469
00:35:16,967 --> 00:35:22,029
row allows more misbehaviour than the last, so it's harder to tolerate. Here's a

470
00:35:22,029 --> 00:35:23,475
real case for each.

471
00:35:23,975 --> 00:35:29,140
Fail-stop: the part halts, and everyone is reliably told. It's an idealisation, approximated by

472
00:35:29,140 --> 00:35:34,305
fencing: a power switch cuts a suspect node off, so the rest know for

473
00:35:34,305 --> 00:35:35,412
sure it's down.

474
00:35:35,912 --> 00:35:40,982
Crash-stop: the process halts silently, say killed by the operating system for using too

475
00:35:40,982 --> 00:35:46,051
much memory. Nobody is told, and a dead node looks exactly like a slow

476
00:35:46,051 --> 00:35:51,120
one. Majority quorums need two f plus one nodes to survive f crashes: three

477
00:35:51,120 --> 00:35:51,844
survive one.

478
00:35:52,344 --> 00:35:57,926
Crash-recovery: the node crashes and restarts with only what it saved to disk. A

479
00:35:57,926 --> 00:36:03,508
server loses power, reboots, and replays its write-ahead log. Anything it acknowledged but held

480
00:36:03,508 --> 00:36:07,894
only in memory is gone, so write durably before you acknowledge.

481
00:36:08,394 --> 00:36:14,018
Omission: the node keeps running but drops some messages. A full network buffer silently

482
00:36:14,018 --> 00:36:19,642
loses a payment-done message. The sender retries, so the handler must be idempotent, or

483
00:36:19,642 --> 00:36:21,249
the customer pays twice.

484
00:36:21,749 --> 00:36:27,752
Timing: right answers, too late. A database node freezes for ten seconds in a

485
00:36:27,752 --> 00:36:33,756
garbage-collection pause. Its leader lease expires, another node takes over, and the old one

486
00:36:33,756 --> 00:36:38,473
wakes up still thinking it leads. Defences: timeouts, deadlines, careful leases.

487
00:36:38,973 --> 00:36:44,438
Byzantine: anything at all, even lies. In twenty oh eight, an Amazon S-three outage

488
00:36:44,438 --> 00:36:49,903
of several hours was traced to a single flipped bit in the servers' internal

489
00:36:49,903 --> 00:36:55,368
status messages. Defences are checksums and signatures, and full Byzantine tolerance needs three f

490
00:36:55,368 --> 00:37:00,833
plus one replicas. That's why Raft survives one crash with three nodes, but a

491
00:37:00,833 --> 00:37:02,395
Byzantine protocol needs four.

492
00:37:03,295 --> 00:37:08,649
Most data centres assume crash-recovery, omission and timing faults, and add checksums for corrupted

493
00:37:08,649 --> 00:37:14,003
data. Then you handle a fault in three moves. Say the thumbnail service starts

494
00:37:14,003 --> 00:37:19,357
hanging. Detect: a health check every five seconds fails three times in a row.

495
00:37:19,857 --> 00:37:24,571
Isolate: app servers call it with a two-second timeout. Without that, a dependency taking

496
00:37:24,571 --> 00:37:29,286
thirty seconds to fail ties up every thread, and the whole app hangs. A

497
00:37:29,286 --> 00:37:31,981
fast error is better than a slow one.

498
00:37:32,481 --> 00:37:38,569
Recover: route around the bad copies and restart them. Uploads still work. Thumbnails are

499
00:37:38,569 --> 00:37:39,438
just late.

500
00:37:39,938 --> 00:37:45,425
And watch for correlated failure: one bad deploy breaks every copy at once. Netflix's

501
00:37:45,425 --> 00:37:50,912
Chaos Monkey randomly kills production servers so teams have to build for this. Recap:

502
00:37:50,912 --> 00:37:54,048
faults happen to parts, failures to the system.

503
00:37:54,948 --> 00:37:57,862
Redundancy means a spare for each critical part.

504
00:37:58,362 --> 00:38:03,937
A single point of failure, a spof, is any part whose loss alone stops

505
00:38:03,937 --> 00:38:09,512
everything: one database, one load balancer, one D-N-S provider, even one engineer who knows

506
00:38:09,512 --> 00:38:10,309
the password.

507
00:38:10,809 --> 00:38:15,622
Failover moves work to the spare, like a hospital switching to backup generators within

508
00:38:15,622 --> 00:38:16,997
seconds of a blackout.

509
00:38:17,497 --> 00:38:22,605
To find spofs, ask of every box: if this disappears, what happens? Let's do

510
00:38:22,605 --> 00:38:24,430
that to the photo app.

511
00:38:25,330 --> 00:38:31,227
D-N-S. If our one D-N-S provider goes down, nobody can find the app at

512
00:38:31,227 --> 00:38:36,281
all. Fix: a second provider, or a managed service that's redundant inside.

513
00:38:36,781 --> 00:38:41,996
Load balancer. A single one is a spof. Run a pair, or use a

514
00:38:41,996 --> 00:38:42,740
managed one.

515
00:38:43,240 --> 00:38:48,943
App servers. We run three across zones. Lose one and the others carry on.

516
00:38:48,943 --> 00:38:50,165
Not a spof.

517
00:38:50,665 --> 00:38:57,252
Database. One primary. If it dies, every upload and every cache miss fails. That's

518
00:38:57,252 --> 00:39:02,897
our biggest spof. Fix: a standby in another zone with automatic failover.

519
00:39:03,397 --> 00:39:08,300
Cache. If it dies, nothing is lost, but all fifteen thousand peak reads hit

520
00:39:08,300 --> 00:39:12,503
the database at once and swamp it. So run cache replicas too.

521
00:39:13,003 --> 00:39:19,041
Object storage. A managed service that already copies data across zones. Fine. Common mistake:

522
00:39:19,041 --> 00:39:25,079
making app servers redundant but leaving one database, or putting all the copies in

523
00:39:25,079 --> 00:39:25,941
one rack.

524
00:39:26,841 --> 00:39:33,330
Two ways to run the copies. Active-passive: a primary serves everything and replicates to

525
00:39:33,330 --> 00:39:39,818
an idle standby. Simple, but failover takes seconds to minutes. It suits single-writer databases,

526
00:39:39,818 --> 00:39:41,672
like our photo database.

527
00:39:42,172 --> 00:39:48,330
Active-active: all nodes serve, and survivors absorb a failure. Better use of hardware and

528
00:39:48,330 --> 00:39:54,489
faster recovery, but hard for databases, where two writers can conflict. It suits stateless

529
00:39:54,489 --> 00:39:56,688
services, like our app servers.

530
00:39:57,588 --> 00:40:03,146
Standbys come in three temperatures. Let's apply each to the photo database dying at

531
00:40:03,146 --> 00:40:03,940
eight p.m.

532
00:40:04,440 --> 00:40:09,993
Cold standby: the spare is only last night's backup. Someone restores it onto a

533
00:40:09,993 --> 00:40:15,545
fresh machine. That takes hours, and every photo since the backup is lost. Cheap,

534
00:40:15,545 --> 00:40:17,925
and fine for an internal tool.

535
00:40:18,425 --> 00:40:23,482
Warm standby: a replica is already running, but copying a few minutes behind. Promote

536
00:40:23,482 --> 00:40:28,539
it and you're back in minutes, maybe losing the last few seconds of writes.

537
00:40:29,039 --> 00:40:34,850
Hot standby: a replica fully in sync, confirming every write before the primary acknowledges

538
00:40:34,850 --> 00:40:40,662
it. Failover is automatic, in seconds, losing nothing. A real managed example: Amazon R-D-S

539
00:40:40,662 --> 00:40:46,474
Multi-A-Z keeps a synchronous standby in another zone and fails over on its own,

540
00:40:46,474 --> 00:40:48,965
typically within a minute or two.

541
00:40:49,465 --> 00:40:55,192
How many spares? If N servers handle peak, run N plus one to survive

542
00:40:55,192 --> 00:41:00,919
a failure: if thirteen handle peak, run fourteen. N plus two, fifteen, survives a

543
00:41:00,919 --> 00:41:02,146
failure during maintenance.

544
00:41:02,646 --> 00:41:08,169
Two warnings. Split brain: if a slow primary is wrongly declared dead, two primaries

545
00:41:08,169 --> 00:41:13,691
accept writes and the data forks. And test failover, because untested failover fails when

546
00:41:13,691 --> 00:41:14,875
you need it.

547
00:41:15,775 --> 00:41:20,934
Scalability is handling more load by adding resources, without a redesign and without collapsing.

548
00:41:20,934 --> 00:41:26,094
A restaurant that serves more diners by adding tables and cooks scales. One whose

549
00:41:26,094 --> 00:41:29,780
tiny kitchen tops out at fifty meals an hour doesn't.

550
00:41:30,280 --> 00:41:35,392
Always name the load parameter and the factor. Not, it's scalable. Say: we can

551
00:41:35,392 --> 00:41:39,408
go to ten times the requests per second by adding servers.

552
00:41:39,908 --> 00:41:44,704
Four dimensions, shown on the photo app. Load: peak reads grow from fifteen thousand

553
00:41:44,704 --> 00:41:47,445
to a hundred and fifty thousand a second.

554
00:41:47,945 --> 00:41:52,531
Data: one terabyte a day becomes nearly two petabytes in five years.

555
00:41:53,031 --> 00:41:57,454
Geography: users in Sydney want the feed as fast as users in Virginia.

556
00:41:57,954 --> 00:42:04,295
Organisation: the team grows from five engineers to fifty, who must ship without stepping

557
00:42:04,295 --> 00:42:10,635
on each other. Techniques: stateless servers, caches, replicas, sharding, queues and C-D-Ns. Scale the

558
00:42:10,635 --> 00:42:16,069
real bottleneck, and make the next ten times easy, not a thousand.

559
00:42:16,969 --> 00:42:24,208
Vertical scaling, scaling up, makes one machine bigger: a bigger bus. Horizontal scaling, scaling

560
00:42:24,208 --> 00:42:26,793
out, adds machines: more buses.

561
00:42:27,293 --> 00:42:32,353
Scaling up is often just a config change in the cloud. Scaling out needs

562
00:42:32,353 --> 00:42:37,413
work you can divide. Stateless app servers are easy. Database writes need sharding: each

563
00:42:37,413 --> 00:42:39,581
machine holds part of the data.

564
00:42:40,481 --> 00:42:46,308
Let's grow the photo app. At launch, a thousand users: one machine with four

565
00:42:46,308 --> 00:42:50,469
cores and sixteen gigabytes runs everything. That's correct, not lazy.

566
00:42:50,969 --> 00:42:56,733
At a hundred thousand users, it's busy. Scale up: stop it, pick a sixty-four

567
00:42:56,733 --> 00:43:02,496
core, five hundred gigabyte machine, start it. A few minutes of downtime, zero code

568
00:43:02,496 --> 00:43:02,908
changes.

569
00:43:03,408 --> 00:43:08,657
At a million users, one machine is a spof. Scale out the app tier:

570
00:43:08,657 --> 00:43:13,907
three stateless servers behind a load balancer. Keep one big database, plus a standby.

571
00:43:14,407 --> 00:43:19,519
At ten million users, the database is the limit. Send reads to replicas and

572
00:43:19,519 --> 00:43:24,267
a cache. That's out for reads, but writes still go to one primary.

573
00:43:24,767 --> 00:43:31,056
Beyond that, writes outgrow the biggest machine. Now shard: split users across several databases.

574
00:43:31,056 --> 00:43:34,650
That's real redesign work, so you delay it.

575
00:43:35,550 --> 00:43:38,116
Up is simple, with no code change.

576
00:43:38,616 --> 00:43:42,180
But it has a hard ceiling: the biggest machine you can buy.

577
00:43:42,680 --> 00:43:46,999
And it's still a spof, with big boxes costing disproportionately more.

578
00:43:47,499 --> 00:43:49,392
Out is practically unlimited.

579
00:43:49,892 --> 00:43:53,665
Losing a node loses only a fraction, and you add nodes live.

580
00:43:54,165 --> 00:43:59,666
But it needs statelessness or sharding, and consistency gets harder. Don't dismiss scaling up:

581
00:43:59,666 --> 00:44:05,167
Stack Overflow in twenty sixteen ran a huge site on eleven web servers and

582
00:44:05,167 --> 00:44:06,345
four database servers.

583
00:44:07,245 --> 00:44:12,842
Elasticity is scaling up and down automatically as load changes, so you pay for

584
00:44:12,842 --> 00:44:18,439
roughly what you use. Scalability says we can grow. Elasticity says we grow and

585
00:44:18,439 --> 00:44:23,236
shrink by ourselves, like a supermarket opening checkouts when queues get long.

586
00:44:23,736 --> 00:44:30,228
Autoscaling watches a metric, like C-P-U, requests per second or queue length. Above a

587
00:44:30,228 --> 00:44:36,721
threshold, add servers. Below another, remove some. Serverless platforms can even scale to zero.

588
00:44:37,221 --> 00:44:43,435
The limits: containers start in seconds, virtual machines in minutes, so a sudden spike

589
00:44:43,435 --> 00:44:49,206
can outrun you. Mostly stateless parts are elastic. And over-eager scale-down causes flapping.

590
00:44:50,106 --> 00:44:54,935
An online shop normally runs ten app servers at about forty percent C-P-U.

591
00:44:55,435 --> 00:45:00,449
At eight p.m. the Black Friday sale opens and traffic jumps four times in

592
00:45:00,449 --> 00:45:05,463
a few minutes. C-P-U passes seventy percent and stays there for five minutes, so

593
00:45:05,463 --> 00:45:06,896
the autoscaler adds servers.

594
00:45:07,396 --> 00:45:12,446
Containers start in seconds, and the fleet grows to about thirty, with C-P-U near

595
00:45:12,446 --> 00:45:17,497
sixty percent. Virtual machines taking minutes would have left users with a slow first

596
00:45:17,497 --> 00:45:18,218
few minutes.

597
00:45:18,718 --> 00:45:24,512
After midnight traffic falls and C-P-U drops below thirty percent. The autoscaler removes servers

598
00:45:24,512 --> 00:45:27,822
gradually, with a cooldown so it doesn't flap.

599
00:45:28,322 --> 00:45:34,207
Next year they pre-scale at seven thirty on a schedule, so the spike never

600
00:45:34,207 --> 00:45:40,092
outruns them. In the interview: scalability is the capability, elasticity is doing it automatically,

601
00:45:40,092 --> 00:45:40,932
both ways.

602
00:45:41,832 --> 00:45:46,321
State is data remembered between requests, like who's logged in, or a cart. A

603
00:45:46,321 --> 00:45:50,810
stateless service keeps none of it in its own memory, so any copy can

604
00:45:50,810 --> 00:45:55,298
serve any request, like a call centre where any agent can pull up your

605
00:45:55,298 --> 00:45:59,787
file. A stateful one ties you to one server, like a family doctor who

606
00:45:59,787 --> 00:46:03,314
keeps your history in their head: great until they're on holiday.

607
00:46:03,814 --> 00:46:08,877
On the left, user A's session lives inside server one. On the right, any

608
00:46:08,877 --> 00:46:13,941
server can take the request, because the session lives in a shared Redis. Let's

609
00:46:13,941 --> 00:46:16,472
watch why that matters with one login.

610
00:46:17,372 --> 00:46:22,140
Step one. Priya logs in to the photo app. The load balancer sends her

611
00:46:22,140 --> 00:46:25,886
to server one, which stores her session in its own memory.

612
00:46:26,386 --> 00:46:31,460
Step two. Her next tap goes to server two, which has never heard of

613
00:46:31,460 --> 00:46:36,534
her, so she's back at the login page. Local sessions plus a second server

614
00:46:36,534 --> 00:46:38,709
means random logouts. A classic bug.

615
00:46:39,209 --> 00:46:44,412
Step three, the patch: sticky sessions. The load balancer always sends Priya to server

616
00:46:44,412 --> 00:46:49,614
one. It works, until server one crashes or gets redeployed, and every user on

617
00:46:49,614 --> 00:46:51,844
it is logged out at once.

618
00:46:52,344 --> 00:46:56,802
Step four, the fix. Server one puts the session in Redis and gives Priya

619
00:46:56,802 --> 00:47:01,260
a cookie with just a session I-D. Any server looks it up in about

620
00:47:01,260 --> 00:47:02,216
half a millisecond.

621
00:47:02,716 --> 00:47:08,331
Step five, the other fix: a signed token, a J-W-T, holding her user I-D.

622
00:47:08,331 --> 00:47:12,342
Any server checks the signature, with no lookup at all.

623
00:47:12,842 --> 00:47:17,689
Now kill any server and Priya doesn't notice. The cost is that small extra

624
00:47:17,689 --> 00:47:22,536
hop, and Redis now needs its own spares, because the state moved, it didn't

625
00:47:22,536 --> 00:47:27,382
vanish. Game and chat servers stay stateful by nature, so they use sticky sessions

626
00:47:27,382 --> 00:47:29,459
and plan for that server dying.

627
00:47:30,359 --> 00:47:33,378
Performance is speed for one request at the current load.

628
00:47:33,878 --> 00:47:35,968
It's about latency and efficiency.

629
00:47:36,468 --> 00:47:43,168
Diagnostic: send one request to an idle system. If even that's slow, it's performance.

630
00:47:43,668 --> 00:47:46,989
Scalability is whether that speed holds as load grows.

631
00:47:47,489 --> 00:47:51,030
Fast alone but slow under traffic: that's scalability.

632
00:47:51,530 --> 00:47:56,402
A sports car is fast for one person but seats two. A train is

633
00:47:56,402 --> 00:48:00,926
slower off the mark but carries a thousand. Let's diagnose two real-looking complaints.

634
00:48:01,826 --> 00:48:07,020
Complaint one: the feed takes two seconds even at three a.m., with almost no

635
00:48:07,020 --> 00:48:12,213
traffic. Even one request is slow, so it's performance. Profiling shows a query scanning

636
00:48:12,213 --> 00:48:17,407
all ten million photos because a column has no index. Add the index and

637
00:48:17,407 --> 00:48:21,488
it takes about five milliseconds. More servers would have done nothing.

638
00:48:21,988 --> 00:48:27,212
Complaint two: the feed takes a hundred milliseconds alone, but three seconds at the

639
00:48:27,212 --> 00:48:32,436
evening peak. Fast alone, slow under load, so it's scalability. The database sits at

640
00:48:32,436 --> 00:48:36,167
a hundred percent, so add a cache and read replicas.

641
00:48:36,667 --> 00:48:41,783
And one change can trade them: a hop to a shared cache costs every

642
00:48:41,783 --> 00:48:46,898
request half a millisecond, but lets you scale out. Recap: diagnose which one before

643
00:48:46,898 --> 00:48:47,629
you fix.

644
00:48:48,529 --> 00:48:53,481
Concurrency is many tasks in progress over the same period, interleaved while each one

645
00:48:53,481 --> 00:48:53,835
waits.

646
00:48:54,335 --> 00:48:57,736
It's about how work is organised, and one core is enough.

647
00:48:58,236 --> 00:49:03,138
Rob Pike, a co-creator of Go, put it this way: concurrency is dealing with

648
00:49:03,138 --> 00:49:04,889
lots of things at once.

649
00:49:05,389 --> 00:49:08,314
Parallelism is tasks running at the very same instant.

650
00:49:08,814 --> 00:49:12,669
It's about execution, and needs multiple cores or machines.

651
00:49:13,169 --> 00:49:17,379
Parallelism is doing lots of things at once. Let's see both in a kitchen,

652
00:49:17,379 --> 00:49:18,883
then in a web server.

653
00:49:19,783 --> 00:49:25,055
The kitchen. One cook puts pasta on to boil for ten minutes, and meanwhile

654
00:49:25,055 --> 00:49:30,328
chops the salad and plates a dessert. One person, three dishes in progress: concurrency.

655
00:49:30,828 --> 00:49:35,857
Hire three cooks, each making a dish at the same moment. That's parallelism.

656
00:49:36,357 --> 00:49:41,928
Now a web server. Each feed request spends fifty milliseconds waiting on the database

657
00:49:41,928 --> 00:49:47,499
and about one millisecond computing. One blocking thread handles one request at a time:

658
00:49:47,499 --> 00:49:52,672
one divided by point zero five is twenty requests a second, mostly waiting.

659
00:49:53,172 --> 00:49:58,620
An event loop sends query A, doesn't wait, starts request B, and finishes each

660
00:49:58,620 --> 00:50:04,069
when its result arrives. The same thread now handles roughly a thousand a second,

661
00:50:04,069 --> 00:50:09,517
limited only by that millisecond of C-P-U. That's how Node and NGINX work. Threads

662
00:50:09,517 --> 00:50:12,241
and goroutines are other routes to concurrency.

663
00:50:12,741 --> 00:50:17,719
Parallelism is for C-P-U-heavy work. Resizing a hundred photos at two hundred milliseconds each

664
00:50:17,719 --> 00:50:22,697
takes twenty seconds on one core, and about two and a half seconds across

665
00:50:22,697 --> 00:50:23,052
eight.

666
00:50:23,552 --> 00:50:28,474
But Amdahl's law caps it. If ten percent of a job must run serially,

667
00:50:28,474 --> 00:50:31,286
even infinite cores give at most ten times.

668
00:50:32,186 --> 00:50:37,271
Shared data brings hazards. A race condition: two withdrawals of ten both read a

669
00:50:37,271 --> 00:50:41,267
balance of a hundred, both write ninety, and one withdrawal vanishes.

670
00:50:41,767 --> 00:50:47,582
Deadlock: task one holds lock A and waits for B, task two holds B

671
00:50:47,582 --> 00:50:52,566
and waits for A, forever. Locks, atomic operations and transactions handle these.

672
00:50:53,066 --> 00:50:59,592
Rules: use concurrency for I-O-bound work that mostly waits. Use parallelism for C-P-U-bound work,

673
00:50:59,592 --> 00:51:06,117
with no more C-P-U threads than cores. Interview question: is single-threaded Node concurrent? Yes,

674
00:51:06,117 --> 00:51:12,643
through its event loop. But not parallel for JavaScript, so run several processes for

675
00:51:12,643 --> 00:51:13,576
more cores.

676
00:51:14,476 --> 00:51:19,954
A bottleneck is the part that limits capacity, like a bottle's narrow neck. Fixing

677
00:51:19,954 --> 00:51:25,433
anything else won't help. Each of the four resources has its own symptom. C-P-U:

678
00:51:25,433 --> 00:51:28,564
near a hundred percent, latency rising with load.

679
00:51:29,064 --> 00:51:34,439
Memory: swapping to disk, out-of-memory crashes, or a cache whose hit rate falls.

680
00:51:34,939 --> 00:51:39,792
Disk: high wait times, with I-O-P-S or megabytes per second at their limit.

681
00:51:40,292 --> 00:51:43,241
Network: a saturated link and dropped packets.

682
00:51:44,141 --> 00:51:50,515
C-P-U. Someone made app servers resize photos inside the upload request. C-P-U pins at

683
00:51:50,515 --> 00:51:56,889
a hundred percent and uploads take four seconds. Fix: move resizing to workers on

684
00:51:56,889 --> 00:51:57,799
the queue.

685
00:51:58,299 --> 00:52:03,499
Memory. The cache box has sixty-four gigabytes, but the hot set has grown to

686
00:52:03,499 --> 00:52:08,699
eighty. The hit rate falls from ninety-five to seventy percent, so misses go from

687
00:52:08,699 --> 00:52:13,898
five to thirty percent. That's six times the database load. Fix: more cache memory

688
00:52:13,898 --> 00:52:15,013
or more nodes.

689
00:52:15,513 --> 00:52:21,650
Disk. The database's cloud disk allows three thousand I-O-P-S, but cache misses now need

690
00:52:21,650 --> 00:52:27,788
five thousand random reads a second. Disk wait climbs, and p ninety-nine with it,

691
00:52:27,788 --> 00:52:33,925
while C-P-U looks idle. Fix: buy more I-O-P-S, move to N-V-M-e, or fix the

692
00:52:33,925 --> 00:52:34,802
cache first.

693
00:52:35,302 --> 00:52:41,970
Network. Twenty gigabits a second of photos through ten gigabit cards. Links saturate and

694
00:52:41,970 --> 00:52:44,351
packets drop. Fix: a C-D-N.

695
00:52:45,251 --> 00:52:51,194
Disks have two separate limits. I-O-P-S counts small random operations per second, like lookups.

696
00:52:51,194 --> 00:52:57,136
Throughput counts megabytes per second for big sequential transfers. A hard disk does about

697
00:52:57,136 --> 00:53:02,230
seventy-five to a hundred and fifty I-O-P-S, because the arm must move.

698
00:53:02,730 --> 00:53:07,699
A SATA S-S-D: tens of thousands, at about five hundred megabytes a second.

699
00:53:08,199 --> 00:53:11,473
N-V-M-e: hundreds of thousands to about a million.

700
00:53:11,973 --> 00:53:16,936
A cloud g-p-three disk starts at three thousand I-O-P-S and a hundred and twenty-five

701
00:53:16,936 --> 00:53:21,899
megabytes per second. So twenty thousand random reads a second needs over a hundred

702
00:53:21,899 --> 00:53:24,026
hard disks, or one N-V-M-e drive.

703
00:53:24,926 --> 00:53:28,840
Step one, measure each resource under load. Don't guess.

704
00:53:29,340 --> 00:53:34,430
Step two, look for the one at its limit, or a queue growing in

705
00:53:34,430 --> 00:53:39,521
front of something: a full thread pool, exhausted database connections, lock waits. Also hot

706
00:53:39,521 --> 00:53:40,975
rows and rate limits.

707
00:53:41,475 --> 00:53:46,747
Step three, fix it and measure again. The bottleneck moves. Fix the cache and

708
00:53:46,747 --> 00:53:48,630
the network may be next.

709
00:53:49,130 --> 00:53:54,712
Pause: latency rises under load, but C-P-U is at thirty percent. Where do you

710
00:53:54,712 --> 00:53:55,111
look?

711
00:53:55,611 --> 00:54:02,789
Elsewhere: disk, network, a pool, a lock, a slow dependency. Find where requests queue.

712
00:54:02,789 --> 00:54:05,866
Most web services are I-O bound.

713
00:54:06,766 --> 00:54:13,478
Last concept: the interview itself. Forty-five to sixty open-ended minutes. A repeatable seven-step structure

714
00:54:13,478 --> 00:54:15,395
shows how you think.

715
00:54:15,895 --> 00:54:23,253
Requirements, five minutes. Estimates, three to five. A-P-I, five. Data model, five. High-level design,

716
00:54:23,253 --> 00:54:30,610
ten. Deep dives, ten to fifteen. Trade-offs and wrap-up, three to five. Let's run

717
00:54:30,610 --> 00:54:35,865
all seven on a classic question: design a U-R-L shortener.

718
00:54:36,765 --> 00:54:43,049
Step one, requirements. Never start by drawing. Functional, what it does: shorten a long

719
00:54:43,049 --> 00:54:49,334
link, redirect, expire after a year. Non-functional, how well: a hundred million new links

720
00:54:49,334 --> 00:54:55,618
a month, redirects under fifty milliseconds at p ninety-nine, four nines, never lose a

721
00:54:55,618 --> 00:54:58,761
link. Out of scope: analytics and accounts.

722
00:54:59,261 --> 00:55:04,238
Step two, estimates. A month is about two point six million seconds, so a

723
00:55:04,238 --> 00:55:09,215
hundred million links is about forty writes a second. At a hundred reads per

724
00:55:09,215 --> 00:55:14,193
write: four thousand redirects a second, twelve thousand at peak. At five hundred bytes

725
00:55:14,193 --> 00:55:19,170
a link, that's six hundred gigabytes a year. One database fits, and a hundred

726
00:55:19,170 --> 00:55:21,303
to one reads means caching matters.

727
00:55:21,803 --> 00:55:27,233
Step three, the A-P-I. POST slash links with the long U-R-L returns a short

728
00:55:27,233 --> 00:55:32,662
code. GET slash code returns a three oh one redirect to the long U-R-L.

729
00:55:33,162 --> 00:55:38,650
Step four, the data model. One links table: short code as the primary key,

730
00:55:38,650 --> 00:55:42,569
long U-R-L, expiry. Every read is a lookup by key.

731
00:55:43,069 --> 00:55:48,966
Step five, the high-level design: load balancer, stateless app servers, cache, database. Walk one

732
00:55:48,966 --> 00:55:54,863
write: make a code, insert the row, return it. Walk one read: check the

733
00:55:54,863 --> 00:55:58,653
cache, on a miss read the database, then redirect.

734
00:55:59,153 --> 00:56:04,341
Step six, deep dives. Codes without collisions: seven characters from sixty-two letters and digits

735
00:56:04,341 --> 00:56:09,530
gives about three and a half trillion codes, from a counter or random with

736
00:56:09,530 --> 00:56:14,718
a uniqueness check. And a celebrity's viral link stays in the cache, so it

737
00:56:14,718 --> 00:56:16,200
never hammers the database.

738
00:56:16,700 --> 00:56:21,882
Step seven, trade-offs and wrap-up. The cache can serve a deleted link for a

739
00:56:21,882 --> 00:56:27,064
minute. The single database primary is a spof, so add a hot standby. At

740
00:56:27,064 --> 00:56:32,246
ten times the load, shard by short code. Then check the design against the

741
00:56:32,246 --> 00:56:33,727
requirements you wrote down.

742
00:56:34,627 --> 00:56:42,716
Make each requirement measurable and name its lever. Availability: percent successful requests. Lever: redundancy

743
00:56:42,716 --> 00:56:43,872
and failover.

744
00:56:44,372 --> 00:56:50,910
Latency: p ninety-nine per endpoint. Lever: caches, C-D-Ns, fewer trips.

745
00:56:51,410 --> 00:56:57,611
Scalability: peak Q-P-S. Lever: stateless services, sharding, queues.

746
00:56:58,111 --> 00:57:04,410
Consistency: after a write, who sees it, and how soon? For the shortener: a

747
00:57:04,410 --> 00:57:08,459
new link should work immediately. Lever: leaders, quorums, consensus.

748
00:57:08,959 --> 00:57:15,474
Durability: never lose an acknowledged write. Lever: logs, replication, backups.

749
00:57:15,974 --> 00:57:24,207
Reliability: error rate and M-T-T-R, via timeouts and retries. Security: authentication and encryption.

750
00:57:24,707 --> 00:57:32,536
Maintainability means operable, simple and evolvable: dashboards per S-L-O, versioned schemas, canary deploys. And

751
00:57:32,536 --> 00:57:40,365
cost per request. Common mistakes: drawing before requirements, naming tech without reasons, and over-engineering

752
00:57:40,365 --> 00:57:42,602
a hundred Q-P-S system.

753
00:57:43,502 --> 00:57:49,500
Design is a loop: goal, load, sketch, weak spots, fix. Built from eight standard

754
00:57:49,500 --> 00:57:52,071
boxes, each justified by a number.

755
00:57:52,571 --> 00:57:59,318
Use percentiles, not averages, and fix the biggest latency term first. Latency explodes near

756
00:57:59,318 --> 00:58:00,282
full load.

757
00:58:00,782 --> 00:58:06,433
Little's law sizes pools. RAM is nanoseconds, a data-centre trip half a millisecond, an

758
00:58:06,433 --> 00:58:09,259
ocean seventy to a hundred and fifty.

759
00:58:09,759 --> 00:58:14,559
Divide per-day counts by ten to the five, then apply peak, utilisation and zone

760
00:58:14,559 --> 00:58:14,902
loss.

761
00:58:15,402 --> 00:58:20,673
Three nines is forty-three minutes a month. One bad afternoon can spend it, so

762
00:58:20,673 --> 00:58:22,556
cut M-T-T-R and remove spofs.

763
00:58:23,056 --> 00:58:29,973
Keep servers stateless, measure bottlenecks, and follow the seven steps. Next episode: networking, from

764
00:58:29,973 --> 00:58:32,938
D-N-S and T-C-P to load balancers.

